/Title (In traditional music theory, these are called secondary dominants. Notice that they don't respect the original key signature - they may contains sharps or flats. Whenever a secondary dominant is used, our ears expect the next chord to be the resolution \(where the arrow points\). Whenever a composer breaks this expectation, we experience a deceptive cadence.  Let's take a look at Beatles' "Yesterday" \(transposed to C major\).)
